00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
the shadow education secretary Richard  Phillipson who joins us now thanks so  much for being with us we just heard  from Nadeem zahari and I showed him some  polling which shows how far ahead the  labor party is but he says it's  kierstama who should be worried right  now do you think that's right  uh no I don't think that's right I mean  we're not complacent about things and  the only pool that matters is the pool  that we see on general election day but  we've made massive progress as a party  and we're the only people setting out a  Clear Vision for how Britain could be  better and Labor's plan would mean we  deliver well-paid highly skilled jobs in  every corner of our country and we get  our economy growing once more something  we haven't seen over these last 12 years  and as you say you know you are doing  well in the polls and so I'm quite Keen  to try and find out a bit more of what  you say is a Clear Vision of what you  know a labor government would look like  what the country under labor would look  like you say you'll deliver more uh jobs  fairness I mean these are things that no  one would disagree with but I'm just  gonna I'm Keen to get a bit more of a  sense about you know the sort of values  and the principles uh that occur Style  with government would entail so would  you spend more money on public services  and than conservatives  well we don't yet know what the  situation will be with the Public  Finance as the conservatives crash the  economy and an incoming labor government  is likely to inherit a very difficult  situation and that will mean we have to  make difficult choices around public  money but we also have choices of course  as well about how we raise money that's  why I believe that we should end the tax  benefits that private schools enjoy and  invest in a brilliant State education  for every child driving up high  standards right across our schools but  you know we're not in a position right  now of course given the very uncertain  nature of the economy to set out exact  figures and what that first Labor budget  would look like  okay I will talk to you about the  education staff and give you the space  to talk about your policy um later in  the interview  um not completely clear from that  question whether you'd spend more on  public services than the conservatives  and how about immigration and we've got  record levels of immigration and nadims  are high we're saying that it's too high  the conservatives want to bring down  particularly illegal migration what's  Labor's position would you like to see  immigration higher or lower  I think my starting point is that  immigration has always been an important  part of our national story as a country  I wouldn't be stood here speaking to you  today we're not for the fact that my  grandparents came to this country from  Ireland worked in the NHS and helped to  build our country but I do recognize at  the same time that we need to make sure  we're investing in the skills of people  here that we're giving everyone the  opportunity to succeed so there is a  balance to strike and that's why we  would maintain a point-space system  where it comes to immigration  okay and that's clear on the  points-based system certainly um but can  we talk about strikes now because again  it's another issue that I think so many  people will have you know big questions  about and public sector workers want a  pay rise to match inflation so inflation  obviously currently around 11 Nadeem  zahari says that would be unsustainable  it'll be unaffordable what is Labor's  position would you give inflation  matching pay Rises to public sector  workers  I completely recognize that right across  our Public Services whether that's  nurses or teachers they've been terribly  let down by this government and they  want a fair deal and that is around pay  but it's also about terms and conditions  the fact that so many people working in  our public services are burnt out feel  that they have a government that is  completely abandoned them under the last  Labor government we made sure that we  invested in our Workforce that we made  sure they were paid properly for the  amazing job that they are doing and I  think that is incredibly important but  as I say pay is one very important  element of this but if you speak to head  teachers or School support staff as I do  right across the country they just feel  you know overworked let down and really  badly demoralized and that's why it'll  be the job of an incoming labor  government to demonstrate that we want  to work with the people the amazing  people in our public services to make  sure that you don't have to wait months  on end to get access to a hospital  appointment the fact that children in  all of our schools have opportunities to  succeed and that we don't end up in this  position as we are now where staff are  having to put their hands in their own  pocket in order to make sure that  children don't go hungry when they  arrive at school and Labor's plan around  breakfast clubs making sure that every  child in our country gets a brilliant  start to the day is part of how we make  life a lot better and easier for staff  working in our Public Services too and I  just want to come back because the  question was specifically about pay  um you say that you would like to see a  fair deal a public sector workers should  be paid properly but but you know the  difficult thing is working out what that  means would you like to see inflation  matching pay rises in the public sector  I want to see a fair deal for all of our  workers right across Public Services of  course this is all about negotiation and  compromise  they want to see a fair deal the  question is what is that don't negotiate  well I do think it means recognizing the  pressure that people are under right now  but also over the last 12 years uh pay  hasn't been keeping up with demands that  workers in our Vital services are facing  but you can't get to a position of  agreement if you're not prepared to even  have that conversation and what I hear  time and again you know whether it's  real or our schools or the Health  Service ministers are just not prepared  to sit down and talk about these issues  properly we won't get an agreement and  we won't get a negotiated settlement but  all sides can live with if ministers  just say well there's nothing to do with  me you know if I was Secretary of State  for Education I'd be around the table  try to get a solution working with trade  unions to find a find a way through all  of this not stalking tension as  ministers all too often seem intent on  doing  um I don't know what um you know we  started the interview talking about the  polls it's pretty clear how well they  were doing with the polls if they were  replicated in a general election like  that result in Chester then you would  have a majority so I get you know that  there is obviously a desire in Labor uh  right now to not scare the horses  effectively to kind of play it safe and  then you'll hopefully get as you say say  uh you know that majority at the next  election but  I guess there's a bit of a frustration  sometimes interviewing people from labor  when it does feel like you're being a  bit reluctant to nail your colors to the  Mast or on public except to pay for  example it's almost like you don't want  to put off either side in the debate by  saying that you want a fair deal you  want everyone to talk but I'm not really  clear on whether or not you think that  inflation matching pay Rises are  unaffordable or if you're saying that  it's something that actually the  government should do whatever happens  foreign  because I think trust in politics  matters and I'm not going to make  promises this morning to you that I  don't feel confident I can deliver I  think that's been what so what's been so  corrosive to our politics in recent  years is Tory politicians in particular  saying one thing and doing another so  yes absolutely I want to see a better  deal for the brilliant people that work  right across our education system and a  part of that is around the choices that  we make around how we find investment  and our policy on private schools around  ending those tax breaks and putting that  1.7 billion pounds into more teachers in  our classrooms into better mental health  support for all of our children and in  making sure that all of our staff are  properly supported that's one I think  really straightforward way the  government right now could be doing  things differently and that's a clear  divide that you see between labor and  the conservatives our focus on high  standards and all of our brilliant State  schools but on driving up standards  further and Richie sunac who isn't  prepared to stand up to the vested  interests in his own party and isn't  prepared to do what's right by children  and families in our country  um you're also calling for a regulatory  investigation into private schools just  to explain what that's about  seen uh for years now are fees going up  often very substantially and you know I  think there needs to be a full  investigation into whether that market  is operating effectively we've heard  Lots about landish claims from Jeremy  Hunt and from Rishi suunak and others  about the impact of Labor's policy which  I just don't think are born out by the  evidence but if you look at the fees  that private schools are charging right  now they have gone up Way Beyond  earnings in cash terms and I think it is  important that the CME investigate this  matter and see whether this Market is  working properly and I think it does now  fall to the Secretary of State for  education to whom I've written to make  sure that matter is referred to the CME  and that they look into it but who's  whose side is she on is she on the side  of parents on the side of children on  delivering a brilliant education for all  of our children or is she not prepared  either the stand up to the vested  interests in our own party  okay thank you very much for being on  the program uh this morning Bridget  Phillips in there for the labor party

